From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 30/30] local: remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_pas: Disable recovery for
 attach-only remoteprocs

A remoteproc using qcom_pas_ops_no_reset (selected by qcom,broken-reset,
added in patch 0013) has no .start op: without a working reset sequence
we cannot boot the remote processor, only attach to one the bootloader
already started. But the remoteproc core's crash recovery does not know
that. Unless RPROC_FEAT_ATTACH_ON_RECOVERY is set (only imx_rproc sets
it), rproc_trigger_recovery() takes the boot path, and rproc_start()
calls rproc->ops->start unconditionally -- a NULL indirect call. An
unprompted DSP crash then oopses the recovery worker and leaves the DSP
offline until reboot, with a dead kworker on top.

Reported against this exact series on an X1P42100 at EL2 (the same
attach + broken-reset stack this kernel runs on x1e80100):

  https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260811171546.188660-1-info@humanlearning.ch/

Fix it the way Stephan Gerhold (author of the series) said he would in
his reply (<anx6j8UX3BzvZ7JN@linaro.org>): mark recovery disabled. We
cannot restart the processor, and attaching to a crashed one makes no
sense, so recovery must fail gracefully -- the crash is still logged and
the remoteproc stays cleanly offline, exactly what happens today minus
the oops.

Drop this patch together with 0013/0014 if the broken-reset approach is
ever replaced (upstream late-attach, or firmware gaining a real reset).
---
 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
@@ -816,6 +816,16 @@
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Without a working reset the remote processor cannot be booted
+	 * again after it crashes: qcom_pas_ops_no_reset has no .start, so
+	 * boot-based recovery would oops in rproc_start() calling it, and
+	 * attaching to a crashed processor makes no sense either. Leave the
+	 * crashed remoteproc offline instead of attempting recovery.
+	 */
+	if (ops == &qcom_pas_ops_no_reset)
+		rproc->recovery_disabled = true;
+
 	rproc->has_iommu = of_property_present(pdev->dev.of_node, "iommus");
 	if (desc->auto_boot) {
 		if (ops->start)
